<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andronikos II Palaiologos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1259">1259</a>–<a href="/years/1332">1332</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Andronicus II Palaeologus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andronikos II Palaiologos was Byzantine emperor from 1282 to 1328. His long reign saw the loss of most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to the Turks and the ravages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-company">Catalan Company</a>, which he had hired. His son Theodore inherited the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a> in 1306 through his mother, and a daughter married John II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>. He was deposed by his grandson Andronikos III.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos.jpg" alt="Andronikos II Palaiologos" width="320" height="553" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Pachymeres · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miniature_of_Andronikos_II.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
